268
00:13:27,674 --> 00:13:30,541
after hours and con
Carl into giving him one.

269
00:13:30,577 --> 00:13:33,777
Are you saying that
Carl's brother killed him?

270
00:13:33,814 --> 00:13:35,913
I'm only saying I didn't!

271
00:13:38,952 --> 00:13:42,286
I saw him; I saw everything.

272
00:13:42,322 --> 00:13:43,621
Okay.

273
00:13:43,657 --> 00:13:47,291
Now, I want to repeat
just what you told me.

274
00:13:47,326 --> 00:13:50,461
Now, you saw a man
come up behind the man

275
00:13:50,496 --> 00:13:52,463
who was decorating the tree

276
00:13:52,498 --> 00:13:55,133
and stabbed him.

277
00:13:55,168 --> 00:13:57,501
Then the man who
was stabbed fell down

278
00:13:57,537 --> 00:13:59,804
and the other man
bent down over him?

279
00:13:59,840 --> 00:14:01,139
Yeah.

280
00:14:01,174 --> 00:14:04,008
Then I started
yelling for my mom.

281
00:14:04,044 --> 00:14:05,909
Well, that's very good, Matthew.

282
00:14:05,946 --> 00:14:08,246
Now, I want you to
do one more thing.

283
00:14:10,583 --> 00:14:15,153
Can you show me the
man who did the stabbing?

284
00:14:15,188 --> 00:14:17,188
That's him.

285
00:14:17,223 --> 00:14:20,024
Well, I'll try to
get you arraigned

286
00:14:20,059 --> 00:14:23,528
by this afternoon and
maybe out on bail by tonight.

287
00:14:23,563 --> 00:14:26,096
Well, hurry up, I
got a store to run.

288
00:14:26,132 --> 00:14:28,076
There is more to Christmas

289
00:14:28,100 --> 00:14:30,802
than running a store
and doing business.

290
00:14:30,837 --> 00:14:32,782
The only reason for
any holiday is to sell.

291
00:14:32,806 --> 00:14:34,283
That's why the government
created most of them.

292
00:14:34,307 --> 00:14:36,941
Now, I-I'm gonna
represent you on this.

293
00:14:36,976 --> 00:14:39,043
I don't know why.

294
00:14:39,078 --> 00:14:41,312
I guess it's because I
know you're too smart

295
00:14:41,347 --> 00:14:43,259
to have committed
a murder this sloppy.

296
00:14:43,283 --> 00:14:45,261
I don't mean that you
wouldn't commit a murder.

297
00:14:45,285 --> 00:14:47,663
I think you would,
but not this one.

298
00:14:47,687 --> 00:14:49,253
I'll tell you something else.

299
00:14:49,289 --> 00:14:51,922
You're the worst
neighbor I've ever had.

300
00:14:51,957 --> 00:14:56,160
And the absolute hatefulest man
I've ever known and the meanest.

301
00:14:56,196 --> 00:14:59,096
Firing Santa Claus
in front of children!

302
00:14:59,131 --> 00:15:02,333
You may not ever know
that this is the season

303
00:15:02,368 --> 00:15:03,880
for giving and being jolly,

304
00:15:03,904 --> 00:15:06,905
but by damn, once in
your life, you're gonna do it.

305
00:15:06,940 --> 00:15:08,584
I don't know
about the jolly part,

306
00:15:08,608 --> 00:15:12,277
but you're gonna give
and I'm talking about<i> give.</i>

307
00:15:12,312 --> 00:15:14,111
Plus my fee.

308
00:15:14,147 --> 00:15:17,148
And an extra 25%

309
00:15:17,183 --> 00:15:19,850
because your dog
dug up my azaleas.

310
00:15:19,886 --> 00:15:21,819
What do you think of that?!

311
00:15:30,096 --> 00:15:31,996
Merry Christmas!

312
00:15:32,031 --> 00:15:35,332
Merry Christmas, everyone!

313
00:15:35,368 --> 00:15:38,068
Ho! Ho! Ho!

314
00:15:38,104 --> 00:15:41,372
Merry Christmas!

315
00:15:44,678 --> 00:15:46,878
I need money, sir.

316
00:15:49,949 --> 00:15:52,250
Merry Christmas!

317
00:15:55,488 --> 00:15:57,888
I need-I need money.

318
00:15:57,923 --> 00:15:59,824
Get away from my store!

319
00:15:59,859 --> 00:16:01,959
This street's as much
ours as it is yours.

320
00:16:01,995 --> 00:16:03,072
Yeah? Yeah!

321
00:16:03,096 --> 00:16:04,456
Yeah, that's right,
they are. Yeah!

322
00:16:08,401 --> 00:16:10,601
Excuse me, sir...

323
00:16:10,636 --> 00:16:13,682
Thank you. Thank
you very much, sir.

324
00:16:31,290 --> 00:16:32,723
Hey, there, sir.

325
00:16:32,759 --> 00:16:34,491
Merry Christmas.

326
00:16:34,527 --> 00:16:35,967
Geez, there's hardly
anybody in here.

327
00:16:35,995 --> 00:16:37,161
It's like a morgue, isn't it?

328
00:16:37,196 --> 00:16:38,441
Yeah. You want something?

329
00:16:38,465 --> 00:16:39,775
You know, I should
buy something.

330
00:16:39,799 --> 00:16:41,966
I should buy that train.

331
00:16:42,002 --> 00:16:43,501
I never had a train before.

332
00:16:43,536 --> 00:16:45,035
You're too old.

333
00:16:47,406 --> 00:16:49,873
Mr. Abbott, I'm
Conrad McMasters.

334
00:16:49,908 --> 00:16:51,375
I work for Ben Matlock.

335
00:16:51,411 --> 00:16:54,412
Two of you, huh?

336
00:16:54,447 --> 00:16:56,292
I thought this was
gonna cost me.

337
00:16:56,316 --> 00:16:58,059
Oh, yeah, there's
a lot in our crowd.

338
00:16:58,083 --> 00:17:00,318
And while it's expensive,

339
00:17:00,353 --> 00:17:02,634
it'll be worth it in the
long run, don't you think?

340
00:17:04,791 --> 00:17:06,469
Why'd you come
back here that night?

341
00:17:06,493 --> 00:17:10,060
I wanted to pick up
the damaged toys

342
00:17:10,096 --> 00:17:13,297
before Carl had a chance to
fix them up and give them away.

343
00:17:13,332 --> 00:17:14,610
Did you come in the
front door or the back?

344
00:17:14,634 --> 00:17:16,734
The back.

345
00:17:16,769 --> 00:17:18,636
So, the front door was locked?

346
00:17:18,671 --> 00:17:21,138
So was the back; I
had to use my key.

347
00:17:22,241 --> 00:17:24,308
So, how did the killer get in?

348
00:17:26,412 --> 00:17:27,857
Maybe he knocked
and Carl let him in.

349
00:17:27,881 --> 00:17:29,080
How should I know?

350
00:17:29,115 --> 00:17:30,148
Who has keys?

351
00:17:30,183 --> 00:17:31,682
Just me and Carl.

352
00:17:31,717 --> 00:17:33,695
So, Carl's wife could've
had a duplicate made.

353
00:17:33,719 --> 00:17:35,852
Yeah.

354
00:17:35,888 --> 00:17:37,132
Carl's sister, Elden
William's wife,

355
00:17:37,156 --> 00:17:38,889
she worked here a while back.

356
00:17:38,924 --> 00:17:40,058
So did Stewart.

357
00:17:40,093 --> 00:17:40,958
Carl's brother.

358
00:17:40,993 --> 00:17:43,393
Yeah, the flake.

359
00:17:43,429 --> 00:17:44,573
Carl used to let them open up.

360
00:17:44,597 --> 00:17:46,108
Both of them could've had keys.

361
00:17:46,132 --> 00:17:47,631
Morning, Mr. Abbott.

362
00:17:48,734 --> 00:17:50,635
How dare you

363
00:17:50,670 --> 00:17:51,802
show your face in here

364
00:17:51,838 --> 00:17:53,381
after what you told
the police about me?

365
00:17:53,405 --> 00:17:54,550
All I did was answer
their questions.

366
00:17:54,574 --> 00:17:56,285
You're fired. Get out, get out!

367
00:17:56,309 --> 00:17:59,042
Get out!

368
00:17:59,078 --> 00:18:01,156
Mr. Abbott, how
could you do that?

369
00:18:01,180 --> 00:18:02,346
It's Christmastime.

370
00:18:02,381 --> 00:18:05,115
She's about as loyal as a snake.

371
00:18:05,151 --> 00:18:08,597
I, for one, say good riddance.

372
00:18:08,621 --> 00:18:11,289
Steve, uh...

373
00:18:11,324 --> 00:18:12,623
I'm so sorry.

374
00:18:12,658 --> 00:18:14,258
Spare me.

375
00:18:14,293 --> 00:18:16,727
I'm Conrad McMasters.

376
00:18:16,763 --> 00:18:18,029
I work for Ben Matlock.

377
00:18:18,064 --> 00:18:19,841
Oh, good, good. And you are?

378
00:18:19,865 --> 00:18:21,999
I'm Carl's brother, Stewart.

379
00:18:22,034 --> 00:18:23,134
Oh, you work here?

380
00:18:23,169 --> 00:18:23,868
Yes. No!

381
00:18:23,903 --> 00:18:24,969
Steve, you need me.

382
00:18:25,004 --> 00:18:26,070
You don't have Carl,

383
00:18:26,105 --> 00:18:28,205
you just fired Donna.

384
00:18:28,240 --> 00:18:29,685
He's not thinking too clearly.

385
00:18:29,709 --> 00:18:31,876
My brother's death
came as a terrible shock.

386
00:18:31,911 --> 00:18:34,045
He and Carl were very close.

387
00:18:34,080 --> 00:18:35,224
I'm staying whether
you pay me or not.

388
00:18:35,248 --> 00:18:37,059
It's the least I can
do for my brother.

389
00:18:37,083 --> 00:18:39,584
Now, where do you want the box?

390
00:18:39,619 --> 00:18:41,986
All right, open it up.

391
00:18:42,021 --> 00:18:43,521
Start stacking those shelves.

392
00:18:43,556 --> 00:18:46,456
$5.50 an hour, you work
only through New Year's.

393
00:18:46,492 --> 00:18:49,326
Those kids are
panhandling out front again.

394
00:18:49,361 --> 00:18:52,163
What? What?

395
00:18:52,198 --> 00:18:53,375
Hey, get away from this store!

396
00:18:53,399 --> 00:18:55,633
Go on, get out!

397
00:18:55,668 --> 00:18:58,969
Um...

398
00:18:59,005 --> 00:19:00,583
I know how upset you must be

399
00:19:00,607 --> 00:19:02,284
losing your brother and all.

400
00:19:02,308 --> 00:19:04,208
Yeah. Yeah.

401
00:19:04,244 --> 00:19:06,255
I hear you were
gonna drop by the store

402
00:19:06,279 --> 00:19:08,724
the night of the murder,
talk to Carl about a job?

403
00:19:08,748 --> 00:19:11,516
Yeah, I was, but
I never got here.

404
00:19:11,551 --> 00:19:13,017
How come?

405
00:19:13,052 --> 00:19:16,721
Well, I... I started over
here and then I thought,

406
00:19:16,756 --> 00:19:18,355
uh, why beg for a job?

407
00:19:18,391 --> 00:19:19,690
I mean, it's the holidays,

408
00:19:19,725 --> 00:19:21,425
party season, so I
figured, you know,

409
00:19:21,460 --> 00:19:23,594
I'd just wait for the weekend,
hit Carl up for a loan.

410
00:19:23,629 --> 00:19:27,030
But Carl's gone,
the cupboard's bare

411
00:19:27,066 --> 00:19:31,035
and $5.50 an hour
looks pretty good.

412
00:19:31,070 --> 00:19:33,171
Where did you go that night?

413
00:19:33,206 --> 00:19:34,839
Uh, movies.

414
00:19:34,874 --> 00:19:36,507
With a girlfriend?

415
00:19:36,542 --> 00:19:38,075
I'm between girlfriends.

416
00:19:38,111 --> 00:19:39,577
Am I a suspect?

417
00:19:39,612 --> 00:19:41,144
Well, it depends.

418
00:19:41,180 --> 00:19:45,149
Now that Carl's dead, who
gets his half of the store, his wife?

419
00:19:45,184 --> 00:19:46,617
No. Sherry, no.

420
00:19:46,653 --> 00:19:47,997
She hates this place
and Carl knew it.

421
00:19:48,021 --> 00:19:50,755
So, he left it to
Kathy, our sister,

422
00:19:50,790 --> 00:19:53,591
and um... me.

423
00:19:53,626 --> 00:19:54,858
You're a suspect.

424
00:20:03,869 --> 00:20:06,770
Those damn kids are
always blocking my doorway,

425
00:20:06,806 --> 00:20:08,884
pestering my customers,
cops never do a thing...

426
00:20:08,908 --> 00:20:10,441
You leaving?

427
00:20:10,476 --> 00:20:11,775
Yeah, I got to talk to Stewart.

428
00:20:11,811 --> 00:20:13,144
Did you buy anything?

429
00:20:13,179 --> 00:20:14,245
No.

430
00:20:14,280 --> 00:20:16,547
Oh. Okay, wait, wait, wait.

431
00:20:16,582 --> 00:20:18,983
How much is the train?

432
00:20:19,018 --> 00:20:21,953
Just the engine is $300.

433
00:20:21,988 --> 00:20:23,955
Here's a ten spot.

434
00:20:23,990 --> 00:20:25,957
Hold it for me, all right?

435
00:20:35,100 --> 00:20:38,001
Did you...

436
00:20:38,036 --> 00:20:39,503
find out anything?

437
00:20:39,538 --> 00:20:41,405
Yeah.

438
00:20:41,440 --> 00:20:42,917
You think you know who did it?

439
00:20:42,941 --> 00:20:44,341
Oh, yeah.

440
00:20:44,377 --> 00:20:47,143
All of them.

441
00:20:48,447 --> 00:20:49,924
Say, do you have
any spare change?

442
00:20:49,948 --> 00:20:51,526
You got a quarter?

443
00:20:51,550 --> 00:20:53,695
A quarter, anything?

444
00:20:53,719 --> 00:20:56,887
Hey, do you have some money?

445
00:20:56,922 --> 00:20:58,456
Hey, thanks.

446
00:20:58,491 --> 00:21:00,402
Oh, man.

447
00:21:00,426 --> 00:21:02,493
Can I have a quarter, sir?

448
00:21:02,528 --> 00:21:06,196
Oh, I've got something
worth a lot more than that.

449
00:21:06,231 --> 00:21:09,666
Do you, uh, oh, do you kids

450
00:21:09,702 --> 00:21:12,503
hang around in front of the
toy store at night sometimes?

451
00:21:12,538 --> 00:21:13,838
You a cop?

452
00:21:13,873 --> 00:21:16,273
No. I'm a lawyer.

453
00:21:16,309 --> 00:21:18,608
What I want to know is,

454
00:21:18,644 --> 00:21:22,813
did you see anybody go in
or come out of the toy store

455
00:21:22,849 --> 00:21:26,117
around say, 7:30
last Tuesday night?

456
00:21:26,152 --> 00:21:29,020
That's when that guy
got offed. That's right.

457
00:21:29,055 --> 00:21:30,988
Well, how much
is it worth to you?

458
00:21:32,091 --> 00:21:36,360
It's worth...

459
00:21:36,395 --> 00:21:38,161
that.

460
00:21:39,532 --> 00:21:41,398
A crummy business card?

461
00:21:41,433 --> 00:21:42,733
No!

462
00:21:42,768 --> 00:21:44,401
That's an I.O.U.

463
00:21:44,437 --> 00:21:47,705
You tell me what you
saw last Tuesday night,

464
00:21:47,740 --> 00:21:50,807
next time you get...

465
00:21:50,843 --> 00:21:52,643
get in trouble with the law,

466
00:21:52,678 --> 00:21:54,378
I'll help you out.

467
00:21:54,413 --> 00:21:55,657
Forget it. We're losing light.

468
00:21:55,681 --> 00:21:57,625
Wait, wait, wait,
wait, wait, wait.

469
00:21:57,649 --> 00:21:59,350
Chill out.

470
00:21:59,385 --> 00:22:01,518
Well, all I have to do

471
00:22:01,553 --> 00:22:03,065
is tell you what
I saw that night,

472
00:22:03,089 --> 00:22:06,857
and I got me a
big-time lawyer? Yeah.

473
00:22:06,892 --> 00:22:09,793
Well, we were out
here all day and all night,

474
00:22:09,829 --> 00:22:11,807
right up till they
carried the body out,

475
00:22:11,831 --> 00:22:13,642
and we didn't see
nothing or nobody.

476
00:22:13,666 --> 00:22:14,832
Still gonna be my lawyer?

477
00:22:16,903 --> 00:22:19,169
Deal's a deal.

478
00:22:19,205 --> 00:22:21,038
How about this?

479
00:22:21,073 --> 00:22:23,207
You keep your
eye on the toy store,

480
00:22:23,242 --> 00:22:24,742
you see anything unusual,

481
00:22:24,777 --> 00:22:26,977
you call me. Huh?

482
00:22:27,012 --> 00:22:29,880
Okay? Okay.

483
00:22:38,390 --> 00:22:41,859
Ah, I love it when Ray
plays those Christmas songs.

484
00:22:41,894 --> 00:22:43,861
Oh, I do, too.

485
00:22:43,896 --> 00:22:46,397
But I gotta eat and go
Christmas shopping.

486
00:22:46,432 --> 00:22:48,099
Stores close in an hour.

487
00:22:49,502 --> 00:22:51,480
I finished my Christmas
shopping in October.

488
00:22:51,504 --> 00:22:53,804
In October?

489
00:22:53,839 --> 00:22:55,339
Yeah.

490
00:22:55,374 --> 00:22:56,840
My mother was like that.

491
00:22:56,875 --> 00:23:00,044
She always finished her
Christmas shopping in October.

492
00:23:00,079 --> 00:23:01,178
Hmm.

493
00:23:01,213 --> 00:23:02,224
All us Calhouns

494
00:23:02,248 --> 00:23:03,681
were like that.

495
00:23:03,716 --> 00:23:06,517
Always finished their
Christmas shopping in October.

496
00:23:06,552 --> 00:23:09,487
In fact, in some places,

497
00:23:09,522 --> 00:23:13,090
we are still known as
the October Calhouns.

498
00:23:13,125 --> 00:23:15,426
October Calhouns.

499
00:23:18,831 --> 00:23:22,767
So, uh, how many presents
you still have to buy?

500
00:23:22,802 --> 00:23:24,769
Oh...

501
00:23:24,804 --> 00:23:26,203
all of them.

502
00:23:26,238 --> 00:23:27,470
All...

503
00:23:27,506 --> 00:23:29,039
You haven't bought any?

504
00:23:29,074 --> 00:23:32,643
No, well, I've got this case,
and I haven't had the time.

505
00:23:32,678 --> 00:23:34,645
Well, I've got time.

506
00:23:34,680 --> 00:23:35,690
Let me do your shopping.

507
00:23:35,714 --> 00:23:37,581
Oh, no, no, no.

508
00:23:37,616 --> 00:23:39,249
What are friends for?

509
00:23:39,285 --> 00:23:42,353
I'm here, I love to shop,

510
00:23:42,388 --> 00:23:44,722
I've got great taste,

511
00:23:44,757 --> 00:23:48,826
and I've already been
an October Calhoun.

512
00:23:48,861 --> 00:23:51,528
So now I'll be a
December Calhoun.

513
00:23:56,769 --> 00:23:58,080
How about you all,
you want another?

514
00:23:58,104 --> 00:23:59,536
No, no, I'm fine.

515
00:23:59,571 --> 00:24:01,738
22,000? That's what I said.

516
00:24:01,774 --> 00:24:03,084
Now, wait a minute,
wait a minute.

517
00:24:03,108 --> 00:24:04,575
Wait, that's too much.

518
00:24:04,610 --> 00:24:05,909
Look...

519
00:24:05,945 --> 00:24:07,945
15 grand

520
00:24:07,980 --> 00:24:12,683
at 20% a week for eight days...

521
00:24:12,718 --> 00:24:14,351
$22,000 today,

522
00:24:14,386 --> 00:24:16,220
$23,000 tomorrow,

523
00:24:16,255 --> 00:24:18,388
$24,000 the next day.

524
00:24:18,424 --> 00:24:19,956
You get the picture?

525
00:24:21,060 --> 00:24:22,326
Yeah.

526
00:24:22,361 --> 00:24:23,961
Good.

527
00:24:28,134 --> 00:24:29,466
Oh.

528
00:24:29,501 --> 00:24:30,868
Oh, hi.

529
00:24:30,903 --> 00:24:32,502
Hi.

530
00:24:32,538 --> 00:24:33,670
Huh.

531
00:24:33,706 --> 00:24:34,771
Hey.

532
00:24:34,807 --> 00:24:36,406
Hey, Mr. Williams.

533
00:24:38,644 --> 00:24:40,555
Your wife told me I
might find you here.

534
00:24:40,579 --> 00:24:41,946
Ben Matlock.

535
00:24:41,981 --> 00:24:44,059
Too bad about
your brother-in-law.

536
00:24:44,083 --> 00:24:45,427
I know how close you were.

537
00:24:45,451 --> 00:24:46,784
Yeah.

538
00:24:46,819 --> 00:24:48,764
Yeah, he was always
loaning you money

539
00:24:48,788 --> 00:24:51,188
and paying off your
gambling debts.

540
00:24:51,223 --> 00:24:52,423
Abbot tell you that?

541
00:24:52,458 --> 00:24:54,068
He told me that
Carl cut you off.

542
00:24:54,092 --> 00:24:55,192
That make you mad?

543
00:24:56,295 --> 00:24:58,762
Abbot killed Carl.

544
00:24:58,797 --> 00:25:00,397
Uh, now that Carl is gone,

545
00:25:00,432 --> 00:25:02,700
who inherits his, uh...
his half of the store,

546
00:25:02,735 --> 00:25:04,367
his wife?

547
00:25:04,403 --> 00:25:06,170
She'll get part of it, yeah.

548
00:25:06,205 --> 00:25:07,238
What about you?

549
00:25:08,807 --> 00:25:09,873
Oh.

550
00:25:09,908 --> 00:25:11,241
You-you're wife told me

551
00:25:11,277 --> 00:25:15,312
that you weren't at home
around 7:30 last Tuesday night.

552
00:25:15,347 --> 00:25:17,982
I was at a friend's watching
the Longo-Robertson fight.

553
00:25:18,017 --> 00:25:19,717
Till when?

554
00:25:19,752 --> 00:25:21,785
Till it was over.

555
00:25:21,821 --> 00:25:24,288
I've got to see somebody.

556
00:25:24,323 --> 00:25:25,855
Another bookie?

557
00:25:29,161 --> 00:25:30,505
How did you do? Pretty good.

558
00:25:30,529 --> 00:25:32,129
Wait, wait, wait,
wait. Count it.

559
00:25:32,164 --> 00:25:34,865
26...

560
00:25:34,900 --> 00:25:37,067
27, 83.

561
00:25:37,102 --> 00:25:40,136
What'll it be, burgers or pizza?

562
00:25:40,172 --> 00:25:41,416
Both.

563
00:25:41,440 --> 00:25:42,551
Couple orders of fries?

564
00:25:42,575 --> 00:25:43,618
Yeah.

565
00:25:43,642 --> 00:25:44,741
And some pop, right?

566
00:25:44,777 --> 00:25:46,076
Yeah, soda pop.

567
00:25:50,749 --> 00:25:52,883
Oh, w-w-w-wait a minute.

568
00:25:52,918 --> 00:25:54,930
That's the door
to the toy store.

569
00:25:54,954 --> 00:25:56,153
Yeah.

570
00:25:56,188 --> 00:25:57,754
Shh. Come on.

571
00:26:01,560 --> 00:26:03,160
Shh.

572
00:26:06,365 --> 00:26:09,805
Come on, come on!

573
00:26:27,687 --> 00:26:29,119
Gone, man.

574
00:26:49,708 --> 00:26:52,709
♪ Away in a manger ♪

575
00:26:52,744 --> 00:26:57,047
♪ No crib for his bed ♪

576
00:26:57,082 --> 00:27:00,950
♪ The little Lord Jesus ♪

577
00:27:00,986 --> 00:27:04,454
♪ Asleep on the hay ♪

578
00:27:04,490 --> 00:27:06,856
♪ He... ♪

579
00:27:06,892 --> 00:27:09,037
So, you think all
of them did it, huh?

580
00:27:09,061 --> 00:27:10,494
Well, they all could have,

581
00:27:10,529 --> 00:27:12,396
and one of them did.

582
00:27:12,431 --> 00:27:15,199
Now, was there a Robertson-Longo
fight on TV that night?

583
00:27:15,234 --> 00:27:18,034
Well, that's easy
enough to find out.

584
00:27:18,070 --> 00:27:20,337
You know, this,
uh, Eldon Williams

585
00:27:20,372 --> 00:27:21,705
is a compulsive gambler,

586
00:27:21,740 --> 00:27:23,652
and I bet he was
just madder than hell

587
00:27:23,676 --> 00:27:25,442
when Carl cut him off

588
00:27:25,477 --> 00:27:27,711
and wouldn't pay his
gambling debts anymore.

589
00:27:27,746 --> 00:27:29,658
You're putting all the red ones

590
00:27:29,682 --> 00:27:31,660
in one place, Conrad.

591
00:27:31,684 --> 00:27:33,150
Well, you can help.

592
00:27:33,185 --> 00:27:34,785
I'm helping.

593
00:27:34,820 --> 00:27:37,220
Hand him that moose.

594
00:27:40,125 --> 00:27:41,525
It's a reindeer.

595
00:27:41,560 --> 00:27:43,093
Thank you.

596
00:27:43,129 --> 00:27:45,874
Stewart Lister is a
real piece of work, too.

597
00:27:45,898 --> 00:27:47,809
He's been hired and
fired so many times,

598
00:27:47,833 --> 00:27:50,501
it's amazing the IRS
hasn't run out of forms.

599
00:27:50,536 --> 00:27:52,135
Oh, that's right.

600
00:27:52,170 --> 00:27:53,982
Carl was taking
care of him, too.

601
00:27:54,006 --> 00:27:55,138
See, now, if Carl

602
00:27:55,173 --> 00:27:56,484
was supporting
both of those guys,

603
00:27:56,508 --> 00:27:59,810
why would either one
of them try to kill him?

604
00:27:59,845 --> 00:28:03,047
Well, they both stood to
inherit part of the toy store.

605
00:28:03,082 --> 00:28:04,415
Okay.

606
00:28:04,450 --> 00:28:06,216
Well, what about his wife?

607
00:28:06,251 --> 00:28:08,185
Grief-stricken.

608
00:28:08,220 --> 00:28:09,564
She hasn't seen anybody

609
00:28:09,588 --> 00:28:11,866
or been out of
the house for days,

610
00:28:11,890 --> 00:28:14,490
but according to her
maid, all that's over.

611
00:28:15,594 --> 00:28:16,860
Why? What happened?

612
00:28:16,895 --> 00:28:18,528
Her roots started to show.

613
00:28:30,843 --> 00:28:32,308
Oh, it's so much better.

614
00:28:32,344 --> 00:28:33,577
<i>Mucho, mucho, mucho</i> better.

615
00:28:33,612 --> 00:28:35,779
Suzanne, you're
a genius. I love it.

616
00:28:37,483 --> 00:28:38,960
Sherry Lister?

617
00:28:38,984 --> 00:28:40,984
Yeah. Michelle Thomas.

618
00:28:41,019 --> 00:28:43,587
I work with Ben Matlock,
Steven Abbot's attorney.

619
00:28:43,622 --> 00:28:45,855
I'm in mourning.

620
00:28:45,891 --> 00:28:47,724
Steven Abbot is the reason why.

621
00:28:47,759 --> 00:28:49,927
You can just keep
on going, Suzanne.

622
00:28:49,962 --> 00:28:51,694
I can appreciate your feelings.

623
00:28:51,730 --> 00:28:53,663
Your husband was a good man.

624
00:28:53,698 --> 00:28:54,998
Generous.

625
00:28:55,034 --> 00:28:57,167
I hear he left his
share of the toy store

626
00:28:57,202 --> 00:28:58,669
to his brother and sister.

627
00:28:58,704 --> 00:29:01,104
He left everything else to me.

628
00:29:01,140 --> 00:29:04,508
I'm not going to be rich,
but I'll be comfortable.

629
00:29:05,711 --> 00:29:07,689
He spend a lot of
time at the toy store?

630
00:29:07,713 --> 00:29:10,180
Every minute he could.

631
00:29:10,215 --> 00:29:11,814
You must have been pretty bored.

632
00:29:12,918 --> 00:29:14,817
I have my own interests.

633
00:29:16,789 --> 00:29:18,988
Steven Abbot says you
were having an affair.

634
00:29:20,092 --> 00:29:22,426
Steven Abbot is a liar.

635
00:29:22,461 --> 00:29:24,105
He says your husband
found out about it,

636
00:29:24,129 --> 00:29:26,764
confronted you,
and eight hours later,

637
00:29:26,799 --> 00:29:28,009
he was dead.

638
00:29:28,033 --> 00:29:29,644
That jerk said
that I killed him?

639
00:29:29,668 --> 00:29:31,580
Maybe Carl was so
mad about your affair

640
00:29:31,604 --> 00:29:33,881
that he threatened to cut
you out of his will completely.

641
00:29:33,905 --> 00:29:36,017
And maybe you killed
him before he could do that.

642
00:29:36,041 --> 00:29:38,242
That's it, blondie.

643
00:29:38,277 --> 00:29:39,287
Where were you that night?

644
00:29:39,311 --> 00:29:40,355
I was out with friends.

645
00:29:40,379 --> 00:29:41,511
What are their names?

646
00:29:44,249 --> 00:29:45,916
Dennis Johnson.

647
00:29:53,258 --> 00:29:54,535
Hey, someone tried to break

648
00:29:54,559 --> 00:29:56,604
into the toy store last
night! Yeah, we chased him,

649
00:29:56,628 --> 00:29:57,906
but he got away.

650
00:29:57,930 --> 00:29:59,263
Did you get a look at him?

651
00:29:59,298 --> 00:30:00,542
It was too dark.

652
00:30:00,566 --> 00:30:02,766
Why didn't you call me?

653
00:30:02,801 --> 00:30:04,834
So, maybe we
didn't have a quarter.

654
00:30:29,261 --> 00:30:30,727
Keep up the good work.

655
00:30:31,830 --> 00:30:33,241
All right, you take this.

656
00:30:33,265 --> 00:30:34,398
Okay.

657
00:30:34,433 --> 00:30:36,177
Get out of here. The
store is not yours to sell.

658
00:30:36,201 --> 00:30:38,368
It belongs to your wife.

659
00:30:39,971 --> 00:30:41,371
What's going on?

660
00:30:41,407 --> 00:30:42,539
Oh.

661
00:30:42,574 --> 00:30:44,741
Eldon here is trying
to sell his wife's share

662
00:30:44,777 --> 00:30:47,310
of the store for half
of what it's worth.

663
00:30:47,345 --> 00:30:48,956
You can't do that.

664
00:30:48,980 --> 00:30:52,048
I, uh... I just wanted
to give you first crack.

665
00:30:52,083 --> 00:30:56,152
I figured I owed it to Carl
to make you a good deal.

666
00:30:56,187 --> 00:30:58,333
Well, you-you can't do that.

667
00:30:58,357 --> 00:31:01,859
He's only asking $2,000 over
what he owes the loan sharks.

668
00:31:01,894 --> 00:31:03,727
Okay. Okay.

669
00:31:03,762 --> 00:31:05,896
Wind up with a total stranger.

670
00:31:05,931 --> 00:31:07,891
Bound to be an
improvement over you.

671
00:31:09,134 --> 00:31:10,745
He... he can't do that.

672
00:31:10,769 --> 00:31:12,469
Hey, wait a minute...

673
00:31:12,504 --> 00:31:13,837
Um...

674
00:31:13,872 --> 00:31:15,405
Uh, guess what?

675
00:31:15,440 --> 00:31:16,973
That, uh... that fight

676
00:31:17,009 --> 00:31:19,609
you said you were
watching that night...

677
00:31:19,644 --> 00:31:21,511
It only went two rounds.

678
00:31:21,546 --> 00:31:23,981
It was over at 7:10.

679
00:31:24,016 --> 00:31:25,349
Say, Steve, uh, seeing...

680
00:31:25,384 --> 00:31:26,816
seeing as business
is kind of slow,

681
00:31:26,852 --> 00:31:28,818
you mind if I...
mind if I take off?

682
00:31:28,854 --> 00:31:30,988
You're off even when you're on.

683
00:31:32,724 --> 00:31:34,802
Have a nice afternoon.

684
00:31:37,930 --> 00:31:39,963
So, business is slow.

685
00:31:39,998 --> 00:31:41,130
Try stinks.

686
00:31:41,166 --> 00:31:43,300
Going to be my worst
Christmas in history.

687
00:31:43,335 --> 00:31:48,071
Well, there's more to
Christmas than just business.

688
00:31:48,106 --> 00:31:49,439
Like what?

689
00:31:50,542 --> 00:31:53,843
Oh, like love,
worship and giving.

690
00:31:53,879 --> 00:31:57,514
But, uh, to get back to
your, uh, original complaint,

691
00:31:57,549 --> 00:32:00,717
your business... I
believe you said... stinks

692
00:32:00,752 --> 00:32:03,052
because of your lousy attitude.

693
00:32:04,289 --> 00:32:06,768
Who do you think
you're talking to? You.

694
00:32:06,792 --> 00:32:09,392
See, if there's two
toy stores in town

695
00:32:09,427 --> 00:32:12,295
and, uh, one of them's
run by a nice guy

696
00:32:12,330 --> 00:32:14,564
and the other
one's run by a jerk,

697
00:32:14,599 --> 00:32:18,201
well, people are naturally
going to go see the nice guy.

698
00:32:18,236 --> 00:32:19,569
See?

699
00:32:19,604 --> 00:32:21,849
Now, you're going to trial

700
00:32:21,873 --> 00:32:24,385
before a jury of
people... just...

701
00:32:24,409 --> 00:32:27,643
just people... And
whether you like it or not,

702
00:32:27,679 --> 00:32:31,447
most of those people
believe that Christmas

703
00:32:31,483 --> 00:32:35,552
is a time of giving,
not just business.

704
00:32:35,587 --> 00:32:38,288
So, unless you want
to alienate these people

705
00:32:38,323 --> 00:32:40,835
and go to prison for
the rest of your life,

706
00:32:40,859 --> 00:32:42,670
you're going to have to hide

707
00:32:42,694 --> 00:32:44,272
your true feelings of Christmas

708
00:32:44,296 --> 00:32:48,398
and make those people
think that you really believe

709
00:32:48,433 --> 00:32:52,369
it is more blessed to
give than to receive.

710
00:32:52,404 --> 00:32:54,070
Now, did you get that,

711
00:32:54,105 --> 00:32:56,806
or do you want me to
run over it one more time?

712
00:33:08,987 --> 00:33:10,487
He snuck up behind him

713
00:33:10,522 --> 00:33:13,423
and stabbed the guy who
was decorating the tree.

714
00:33:13,459 --> 00:33:15,425
Then, the guy fell down.

715
00:33:15,461 --> 00:33:17,427
You know, with
the thing in his back.

716
00:33:17,463 --> 00:33:19,796
Then, the first guy
bent down over him,

717
00:33:19,832 --> 00:33:22,599
like he was going to
stab him again, maybe.

718
00:33:22,634 --> 00:33:24,000
When I started to yell,

719
00:33:24,035 --> 00:33:26,203
the guy looked up and saw me,

720
00:33:26,238 --> 00:33:28,605
then he stopped.

721
00:33:28,640 --> 00:33:31,942
So, you got a real
clear look at his face.

722
00:33:31,977 --> 00:33:33,410
Yeah.

723
00:33:33,445 --> 00:33:36,546
This man that you saw...
Is he in the courtroom now?

724
00:33:36,581 --> 00:33:38,180
Yeah, he's right there.

725
00:33:41,953 --> 00:33:44,687
This is the man that
stabbed Mr. Lister?

726
00:33:45,791 --> 00:33:47,591
You sure?

727
00:33:47,626 --> 00:33:49,926
I'm real sure.

728
00:33:49,961 --> 00:33:52,061
Let the record show the witness

729
00:33:52,096 --> 00:33:54,931
has identified the
defendant, Steven Abbot.

730
00:33:56,034 --> 00:33:57,801
One word, and you'll never hear

731
00:33:57,836 --> 00:34:00,470
the sound of a
cash register again.

732
00:34:02,307 --> 00:34:03,873
Uh, thank you, Matthew.

733
00:34:03,909 --> 00:34:05,208
No further questions,

734
00:34:05,243 --> 00:34:07,410
Your Honor.

735
00:34:07,445 --> 00:34:09,979
Does the defense
wish to cross-examine?

736
00:34:11,083 --> 00:34:12,815
We do, Your Honor.

737
00:34:19,891 --> 00:34:21,068
How you doing, Matthew?

738
00:34:21,092 --> 00:34:23,159
Okay.

739
00:34:23,194 --> 00:34:25,896
Whew-ee, a lot of
people here today.

740
00:34:25,931 --> 00:34:27,463
Is it kind of scary knowing

741
00:34:27,499 --> 00:34:29,466
they're listening to
everything you say?

742
00:34:29,501 --> 00:34:31,201
Kind of.

743
00:34:31,236 --> 00:34:32,846
I'm going to ask you
a couple questions,

744
00:34:32,870 --> 00:34:34,982
and you just answer them
the best you can, okay?

745
00:34:35,006 --> 00:34:36,172
Okay.

746
00:34:37,275 --> 00:34:38,742
Now, you said that man

747
00:34:38,777 --> 00:34:40,010
over there, Mr. Abbot,

748
00:34:40,045 --> 00:34:42,323
came up behind the man
who was decorating the tree,

749
00:34:42,347 --> 00:34:44,081
Mr. Lister, and stabbed him.

750
00:34:44,116 --> 00:34:45,816
Uh-huh.

751
00:34:45,851 --> 00:34:47,984
Who was taller...

752
00:34:48,020 --> 00:34:51,288
The man who decorated the
tree of the man who stabbed him?

753
00:34:52,390 --> 00:34:54,691
I-I-I don't know.

754
00:34:54,726 --> 00:34:56,404
Well, Mr. Abbot had
to have been standing

755
00:34:56,428 --> 00:34:58,339
right behind Mr. Lister
if he stabbed him,

756
00:34:58,363 --> 00:35:00,475
so just think back real hard.

757
00:35:00,499 --> 00:35:03,499
Who was taller...
Mr. Abbot or Mr. Lister?

758
00:35:05,003 --> 00:35:06,469
Mr. Lister?

759
00:35:07,572 --> 00:35:08,771
Hmm.

760
00:35:08,806 --> 00:35:10,418
Maybe this question
will be easier.

761
00:35:10,442 --> 00:35:12,275
Who was heavier?

762
00:35:12,310 --> 00:35:13,743
You know, fatter.

763
00:35:13,778 --> 00:35:16,212
The man who was
stabbed, Mr. Lister,

764
00:35:16,248 --> 00:35:18,315
or the man who
stabbed him, Mr. Abbot?

765
00:35:19,417 --> 00:35:22,418
Um... Mr. Lister?

766
00:35:23,522 --> 00:35:24,654
Hmm.

767
00:35:24,690 --> 00:35:26,556
Well, as it happens,

768
00:35:26,591 --> 00:35:28,058
Matthew...

769
00:35:28,093 --> 00:35:29,959
Mr. Lister was...

770
00:35:29,994 --> 00:35:32,962
only about five foot,
seven inches tall.

771
00:35:34,465 --> 00:35:35,932
Mr. Abbot's...

772
00:35:35,967 --> 00:35:37,934
almost six feet tall,

773
00:35:37,969 --> 00:35:39,936
so he's the taller man.

774
00:35:39,971 --> 00:35:42,439
He also weighs over 200 pounds,

775
00:35:42,474 --> 00:35:45,642
so Mr. Abbot's the
heavier man, too.

776
00:35:45,677 --> 00:35:48,011
Look, here's a
picture of them. See?

777
00:35:48,046 --> 00:35:52,415
See, that's Mr. Abbot,
and that's Mr. Lister.

778
00:35:54,486 --> 00:35:56,686
Guess I made a mistake, huh?

779
00:35:56,721 --> 00:35:58,688
Yeah.

780
00:35:58,723 --> 00:36:01,691
And I think I know why.

781
00:36:01,726 --> 00:36:04,026
It's because you
never saw Mr. Abbot

782
00:36:04,062 --> 00:36:07,029
and Mr. Lister standing up.

783
00:36:07,065 --> 00:36:10,032
All you saw was
Mr. Lister lying on the floor,

784
00:36:10,068 --> 00:36:12,902
and Mr. Abbot bending
over him, isn't that right?

785
00:36:15,006 --> 00:36:17,385
Nobody's gonna be
disappointed in you,

786
00:36:17,409 --> 00:36:20,377
nobody's gonna
be angry with you...

787
00:36:20,412 --> 00:36:23,346
as long as you tell the truth.

788
00:36:25,417 --> 00:36:27,183
What did you see

789
00:36:27,218 --> 00:36:29,458
when you looked in the
window that night, Matthew?

790
00:36:33,658 --> 00:36:37,127
I saw a man lying on the floor.

791
00:36:37,162 --> 00:36:40,597
And I saw that man over
there leaning over him.

792
00:36:40,632 --> 00:36:43,032
So, you never saw

793
00:36:43,068 --> 00:36:45,535
that man over there,
Mr. Abbot, stab anyone?

794
00:36:45,570 --> 00:36:47,370
No.

795
00:36:50,175 --> 00:36:51,808
Nothing further.

796
00:36:56,147 --> 00:36:59,048
♪ ♪

797
00:37:12,363 --> 00:37:14,375
You ain't gettin'
away from us this time,

798
00:37:14,399 --> 00:37:16,366
sucker.

799
00:37:28,780 --> 00:37:29,979
Caught him breaking in!

800
00:37:30,014 --> 00:37:31,459
